name: dotnet-channels

Guide to `System.Threading.Channels` for thread-safe producer/consumer queues in .NET: bounded/unbounded creation, backpressure, `IAsyncEnumerable` integration, and graceful shutdown/draining. For hosted services see [skill:dotnet-background-services]; for async/cancellation patterns see [skill:dotnet-csharp-async-patterns].

A `Channel<T>` has separate `ChannelWriter<T>` and `ChannelReader<T>` endpoints; it handles synchronization.

```csharp
Channel<WorkItem> channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<WorkItem>();
ChannelWriter<WorkItem> writer = channel.Writer;
ChannelReader<WorkItem> reader = channel.Reader;
```

| Aspect | Bounded | Unbounded |
|---|---|---|
| Creation | `Channel.CreateBounded<T>(capacity)` | `Channel.CreateUnbounded<T>()` |
| Backpressure | Yes; `FullMode` controls full-channel behavior | No; grows without limit |
| Memory | Capped at capacity | Can exhaust memory under load |
| Use | Production, untrusted producer rates | Guaranteed-low-volume, prototyping |

```csharp
var bounded = Channel.CreateBounded<WorkItem>(
    new BoundedChannelOptions(capacity: 1000) { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.Wait });
var unbounded = Channel.CreateUnbounded<WorkItem>();
```

`FullMode`: `Wait` blocks `WriteAsync` until space is available (default; reliable backpressure); `DropOldest` discards oldest; `DropNewest` discards newest existing item; `DropWrite` discards incoming item and makes `TryWrite` return `false`.

```csharp
var telemetryChannel = Channel.CreateBounded<SensorReading>(
    new BoundedChannelOptions(500) { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.DropOldest });
var logChannel = Channel.CreateBounded<LogEntry>(
    new BoundedChannelOptions(10_000) { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.DropWrite });
if (!logChannel.Writer.TryWrite(entry)) { /* track overflow */ }
```

Starting in .NET 7, drop modes accept an `itemDropped` callback for metrics, logging, rerouting, or cleanup:
```csharp
var channel = Channel.CreateBounded(new BoundedChannelOptions(100)
    { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.DropOldest },
    itemDropped: (item, writer) =>
    {
        logger.LogWarning("Dropped item due to channel overflow: {Id}", item.Id);
        (item as IDisposable)?.Dispose();
    });
```

Use `await writer.WriteAsync(item, cancellationToken)` for backpressure; `writer.TryWrite(item)` is nonblocking. Multiple producers may call `WriteAsync` or `TryWrite` concurrently without external locking.

Call `Complete()` or `TryComplete()` when no more items will be produced, so consumers detect the end. `TryComplete()` is idempotent; pass an error to propagate failure:
```csharp
writer.TryComplete(new InvalidOperationException("Source failed"));
```

Preferred consumption pattern drains available items before awaiting again, reducing async state-machine overhead:
```csharp
while (await reader.WaitToReadAsync(cancellationToken))
    while (reader.TryRead(out var item))
        await ProcessAsync(item, cancellationToken);
```
For simpler cases, use `ReadAsync(cancellationToken)` then process the item.

Scale processing with multiple consumer tasks; each item is read by exactly one consumer. A `BackgroundService` can run workers with `Task.WhenAll`, read using `WaitToReadAsync`/`TryRead`, create a DI scope per item, call `HandleAsync`, log processing exceptions, and stop on cancellation.

`ChannelReader<T>.ReadAllAsync()` returns `IAsyncEnumerable<T>`:
```csharp
await foreach (var item in reader.ReadAllAsync(cancellationToken))
    await ProcessAsync(item, cancellationToken);
```
It handles waiting/draining and completes when the writer completes and items are consumed. ASP.NET Core minimal APIs can return it directly to stream JSON:
```csharp
app.MapGet("/api/events/stream", (
    ChannelReader<ServerEvent> reader, CancellationToken ct) => reader.ReadAllAsync(ct));
```
With the `System.Linq.Async` NuGet package, compose streams with operators such as `.Where(...)` and `.Buffer(50)`.

For a channel-backed async iterator, start a producer, write feed items, call `TryComplete()` on success or `TryComplete(ex)` on failure, then yield from `ReadAllAsync(ct)`; the reader throws propagated errors.

